    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d740">

                <pb facs="0608" n="492" sameAs="frus:frus1914/pg492"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.00/11749.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Ambassador W. H. Page to the Secretary of State.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram—Extract.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Embassy</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1914-04-28">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">April 28, 1914</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Sir Edward Grey informs me that under instructions from him Garden urged Huerta
                    to accept mediation proposed by South American Republics and Garden has reported
                    that Huerta assents. During Sir Edward’s absence in Paris the Mexican Minister
                    in London sent to the British Foreign Office an unofficial request for British
                    Government to offer mediation, which Grey answered by saying that South American
                    Republics had already done so. But he spoke as if he would be willing to offer
                    good offices of British Government if need should arise.</p>
                <p>I thanked Grey for helpful acts of Minister Carden and Admiral Cradock, which
                    seemed to please him.* * *</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Page</hi></signed>.</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
